      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Antisense oligonucleotides (AONs) are small molecules able to bind to the pre-mRNA and modulate splicing. The increasing amount of intronic mutations leading to pseudoexon insertion in genes underlying inherited retinal dystrophies (IRDs) has highlighted the potential of AONs as a therapeutic tool for these disorders. Here we describe how to design and test AON molecules in vitro in order to correct pre-mRNA splicing defects involved in IRDs."},
